EXT. HARBOUR PLAZA - CLIMATE PROTEST - DAY

Crowd. Cardboard signs. A drum somewhere.

Riley, 20s, in a thrifted raincoat, is holding a sign that says THE SEA IS NOT A METAPHOR. She is also holding two coffees.

Tess, 30s, arrives, out of breath, with a tote bag.

TESS
sorry sorry, the train was

RILEY
it's fine. you're here.

Tess takes the coffee. They stand there. The drum gets louder.

Abe, 60s, in a lab coat under a puffer jacket, pushes through with a megaphone he doesn't know how to hold.

ABE
is this thing on? it's not on.

TESS
it's the red button.

ABE
which one's the red button.

Noor, 19, climbs onto a bench. Everyone looks up. Nothing.

NOOR
(to the crowd)
hey. hi. so. we're like, two hundred people, and the thing behind us

She points. The harbour. The water has pulled back, way too far.

Silence.

RILEY
(muttered)
okay that's new.

A horn BLARES from the ferry terminal. A siren WAILS, thin and far away.

RADIO (V.O.)
(from Gil's handheld, crackling)
all units, all units, tide gauges are reading minus four, I repeat, minus four

Gil, 40s, an event steward in a hi-vis vest, jogs over, radio to his ear.

GIL
okay folks, so, quick thing, we're gonna move uphill. calmly.

ABE
(into the megaphone, finally working)
EVERYONE PLEASE MOVE UPHILL.

TESS
(to Riley, quiet)
this is the thing we put on the signs.

RILEY
yeah.

NOOR
(from the bench, calling out)
GIL, WHICH WAY IS UPHILL?

GIL
that way! the library!

The crowd starts to move. Signs drop. The drum stops mid-beat.

Rain. A single raindrop on a sign.

RILEY
(to herself)
okay okay okay.

She takes Tess's hand. They run.
